SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 309 | Wednesday, March 11, 2026

Good morning.

1) Canada: tentative agreement at 11:40pm, no strike
The railroads and the union reached a deal twenty minutes before the deadline. Terms are not public; the union statement mentions "scheduling predictability," which was the sticking point. Embargoes lift today; the backlog clears by the weekend. Potash and grain shippers can exhale.

2) CPI: transportation services up 0.4% on the month
The freight-relevant pieces were mixed. Motor vehicle insurance keeps rising, and it is now the single biggest cost pressure trucking fleets name in surveys, ahead of drivers. Warehouse and storage services rose 0.2%. Goods inflation overall was flat again.

3) California's drayage zero-emission deadline stays put
The air board declined to delay the rule that bars new diesel drayage trucks from registering in the state's port system. Truck makers say they can deliver; drayage operators say the chargers are not there. Both are true. Expect a fleet-age spike in the port drayage pool this year as operators hang on to the trucks they have.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 14%. Share of US container imports arriving through Gulf Coast ports in February, up from 10% five years ago. Houston is the winner.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

